  "live_records": {
    "q-79848": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79848",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $64u+28v=\\text{gcd}(64,28).$ What is the value of $u+v?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}64 & = & 28\\cdot 2 & + & 8 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 28 & = & 8\\cdot 3 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 8 & = & 4\\cdot 2 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(64,28)=4.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =64-28\\cdot 2 \\\\ 4 & =28-8\\cdot 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =28-8\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =28-(64-28\\cdot 2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =28\\cdot 7-64\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =64\\cdot (-3)+28\\cdot 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=-3$ and $v=7.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=(-3)+7=4.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
