    "q-103258": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103258",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $71u+29v=\\text{gcd}(71,29).$ What is the value of $u+v?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}71 & = & 29\\cdot 2 & + & 13 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 29 & = & 13\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 13 & = & 3\\cdot 4 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(71,29)=1.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =71-29\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & =29-13\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =13-3\\cdot 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =13-3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =13-[29-13\\cdot 2]\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =13\\cdot 9-29\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =[71-29\\cdot 2]\\cdot 9-29\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =71\\cdot 9-29\\cdot 22 \\\\  & =71\\cdot 9+29\\cdot (-22)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=9$ and $v=-22.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=9+(-22)=-13.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
