    "q-79901": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79901",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $70u+31v=\\text{gcd}(70,31).$ What is the value of $u+v?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}70 & = & 31\\cdot 2 & + & 8 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 31 & = & 8\\cdot 3 & + & 7 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 8 & = & 7\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(70,31)=1.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =70-31\\cdot 2 \\\\ 7 & =31-8\\cdot 3 \\\\ 1 & =8-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =8-7 \\\\  & =8-[31-8\\cdot 3] \\\\  & =8\\cdot 4-31 \\\\  & =[70-31\\cdot 2]\\cdot 4-31 \\\\  & =70\\cdot 4-31\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =70\\cdot 4+31\\cdot (-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=4$ and $v=-9.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=4+(-9)=-5.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
